{"assertion":{"kind":"theoretical","text":"Erdős Problem #572: declared status 'open'. Formalized: no. Show that for $k\\geq 3$\\[\\mathrm{ex}(n;C_{2k})\\gg n^{1+\\frac{1}{k}}.\\] Current best: It is easy to see that $\\mathrm{ex}(n;C_{2k+1})=\\lfloor n^2/4\\rfloor$ for any $k\\geq 1$ (and $n>2k+1$) (since no bipartite graph contains an odd cycle). Lazebnik, Ustimenko, and Woldar \\cite{LUW95} have shown that, for arbitrary $k\\geq 3$,\\[\\mathrm{ex}(n;C_{2k})\\gg n^{1+\\frac{2}{3k-3+\\nu}},\\]where $\\nu=0$ if $k$ is odd and $\\nu=1$ if $k$ is even. Prize: no. Tags: graph theory, turan number."},"claim_id":"vcl_f3b5bab6c0472917094dc25e4f8fd4d5af87b478a721aa814547e118107da7ea","conditions":[],"created_at":"2026-06-16T00:00:00Z","evidence":[],"extensions":{"vela.legacy-finding.v1":{"annotations":[],"attachments":[],"confidence":{"basis":"external catalogue record","extraction_confidence":1.0,"kind":"frontier_epistemic","method":"llm_initial","score":0.5},"flags":{"contested":false,"declining":false,"gap":false,"gravity_well":false,"negative_space":false,"retracted":false},"legacy_conditions":{"duration":null,"text":""},"legacy_evidence":{"evidence_spans":[],"method":"erdos_deep","model_system":"n/a","replicated":false,"replication_count":null,"type":"theoretical"},"legacy_links":[],"legacy_provenance":{"authors":[],"doi":null,"extraction":{"extracted_at":"","extractor_version":"","method":"database_import","model":null,"model_version":null},"review":null,"source_type":"database_record","title":"erdos_deep:572","year":null},"previous_version":null,"source_schema":"https://vela.science/schema/finding-bundle/v0.10.0","source_version":1,"updated":null}},"imported_from":{"era":"finding_v0_10","object_id":"vf_d147aaea613dd946","object_root":"sha256:82c1faa41818c09d8ce999dac8496c3b1aa4580187d3f97b0d29829b009d61c1","predecessor_commit":"c236b4fdedb25e0d726e24e2780d50962e4844f7"},"provenance":[{"kind":"database_record","title":"erdos_deep:572"}],"relations":[],"revision":1,"s